{"uuid":"54D3937B-C92F-5483-A127-43B2986429AF","name":"Teriyaki Chicken","serves":10,"duration":10,"cookingDuration":290,"defaultScale":1,"isPublicRecipe":false,"folderIDs":[],"ingredients":[{"uuid":"704CB778-20C7-5AA2-9E50-D10440C13CCF","order":0,"ingredient":{"uuid":"6267B6EE-3B95-5677-A006-DCF72AC0BF9D","name":"chicken breast, whole (not cubed) — package weight; trim before cooking"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"POUND","amount":6}},{"uuid":"825CBF58-9042-568A-832E-FB5932F840B6","order":1,"ingredient":{"uuid":"97510C68-24DF-5489-99C1-7DFE5705687B","name":"bags frozen broccoli florets (~1360 g total)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"ITEM","amount":4}},{"uuid":"D0734C61-9BC4-5F9A-8F5C-2E8E34A22DDE","order":2,"ingredient":{"uuid":"AA47E063-22A0-596A-90E0-655F127B5656","name":"low-sodium chicken broth total (¾ for the sauce, ½ for the crockpot) — opened carton keeps 4–5 days"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"CUP","amount":1.25}},{"uuid":"0319567F-2BA1-5421-9E25-CFFEC7A997B5","order":3,"ingredient":{"uuid":"92CB2AF5-8E39-5C75-A657-7EAA180E80AF","name":"cloves garlic, minced"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"ITEM","amount":9}},{"uuid":"56FC8DE4-D0D9-5E5A-8260-6CA366C08E0D","order":4,"ingredient":{"uuid":"EE00356D-8621-51AF-B0C3-D134C075B961","name":"fresh ginger, minced (or 2 tsp ground — often out at Aldi)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":2}},{"uuid":"1CE034F3-EE4D-510B-BF94-6C0EA7169D08","order":5,"ingredient":{"uuid":"07501558-0B84-5BE2-9346-28BF7F332C17","name":"Scallions and sesame seeds, for eating time (both optional)"}},{"uuid":"77BCEB8A-01DE-579F-8D91-147C5F012ECC","order":6,"ingredient":{"uuid":"DCBA28FC-3BA2-5129-9C7E-DE46B32B1697","name":"jasmine rice (dry)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"CUP","amount":3.25}},{"uuid":"DA014EC0-CDDF-50E0-B563-1A0918EA7429","order":7,"ingredient":{"uuid":"C09C87AA-7455-5CBC-ACA2-5E11F384A06A","name":"low-sodium soy sauce (load-bearing — see Notes)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"CUP","amount":0.6667}},{"uuid":"380F4E59-8F96-588C-923D-7A5F84B14950","order":8,"ingredient":{"uuid":"1C969FF3-B121-5D0F-9A4D-BFDCF1F6CB7A","name":"brown sugar"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":3}},{"uuid":"6F8F1684-67A6-5552-A43F-FF5FEA7C028C","order":9,"ingredient":{"uuid":"E55FC562-D622-5F06-AE91-731B322F4BC1","name":"rice vinegar (or cider vinegar)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":2}},{"uuid":"3C7BBF5F-C8BF-582B-8875-0EF8F82A8D79","order":10,"ingredient":{"uuid":"B8F4E293-2B8F-5587-A09E-0D45E91B0CCC","name":"toasted sesame oil (unreliable at Aldi; a bottle lasts ~5 batches)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":1}},{"uuid":"DB54FF77-A723-589A-B53F-F5E9A963FCE4","order":11,"ingredient":{"uuid":"5FFD8361-BAB6-5E84-9CC2-BFA6B8355515","name":"cornstarch + 4 tbsp cold water"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":2}},{"uuid":"400420CD-5BBD-5805-BCBB-60318960AD22","order":12,"ingredient":{"uuid":"9690C0D1-B8EF-57B5-AD84-5C5781F40DE0","name":"Salt, pepper"}},{"uuid":"BCF3BDCF-843F-5EFA-A7DF-B8E970669BD0","order":13,"ingredient":{"uuid":"201AF182-83DB-5EDB-B6A1-35167A7AC8BD","name":"Sriracha, for eating time"}}],"steps":[{"uuid":"E9531496-0846-5FB4-9EC7-6A9132829EDE","order":0,"step":"Place ~6 lbs whole chicken breasts in the crockpot, trimming any tough or unappealing parts first. Season with salt and pepper and add ½ cup chicken broth. No cutting required.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"3E8D70B2-1313-5FFD-945F-B0B6625A8EA3","order":1,"step":"Cook on High 4–5 hours, or Low 7–8 hours. Do not open the lid before hour 4 — each peek adds 15–20 minutes.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"5B960096-2965-5D43-B0A9-EC2D2C30B0C9","order":2,"step":"Test for shreddability rather than for temperature. Chicken is food-safe at 165°F but will not pull apart until roughly 195–205°F, when the connective tissue breaks down. Pull a piece and try it with two forks; if it holds together in a chunk, give it another 30 minutes.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"8088DCE2-4CF5-5B0E-BBE5-A874DF67AAD8","order":3,"step":"Drain the cooking liquid completely. Unlike the buffalo recipe, none of it is reserved — this sauce is built to its own volume and thinning it with crockpot liquid is what makes teriyaki taste washed out.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"7F652A94-0D4B-5683-808E-70989AF42369","order":4,"step":"Shred the chicken with a hand mixer directly in the crockpot — about 30 seconds. A few pulses only; over-mixing turns it mushy.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"DC360E47-FB8E-52FC-83EA-D19A267721EC","order":5,"step":"Weigh the shredded chicken now, before any sauce goes near it. This is the only moment a clean number exists — once the sauce is stirred in the two cannot be separated, and a sauced weight is not a yield.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"75C246E1-0F6A-54CB-97E3-8F6628A765D3","order":6,"step":"In a saucepan, combine ⅔ cup low-sodium soy sauce, ¾ cup low-sodium chicken broth, the minced garlic and ginger, 3 tbsp brown sugar, and 1 tbsp toasted sesame oil. Simmer 4–5 minutes to cook the raw edge off the garlic.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"E012E914-2BE3-5982-B618-7070242D3700","order":7,"step":"Taste the sauce before thickening. Teriyaki should read savory first with the sweetness behind it. If it tastes like a dessert glaze, it has gone the wrong direction — pull back the sugar next time rather than adding salt to compensate.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"0950BDE0-8DAE-51EA-B30E-5AF2CED87B74","order":8,"step":"Whisk 2 tbsp cornstarch into 4 tbsp cold water, then stir the slurry into the simmering sauce. Cook 1–2 minutes until it coats the back of a spoon. This step is what lets the sauce ride in the container instead of pooling into the rice.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"8AF3B75F-3432-5F81-BC15-93565431F892","order":9,"step":"Remove the sauce from the heat and stir in 2 tbsp rice vinegar. The acid goes in last — simmering it dulls the brightness that keeps this from tasting flat.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"21A7845F-1B3D-5FBC-84D5-4D51919E8511","order":10,"step":"Pour the sauce over the shredded chicken and stir. Hold on Warm 10–15 minutes so it absorbs.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"EACCFC71-E216-5290-B727-FE9BAE280F58","order":11,"step":"During the last ~20 minutes of chicken time, rinse 3 ¼ cups jasmine rice 2–3 times until the water runs mostly clear. Add to the Instant Pot with 3 ¼ cups water (1:1). Measure the water carefully — an over-filled pot gives waterlogged rice that packs soggy.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"282D2C63-0A02-57A8-B89F-E1B2BB1546EE","order":12,"step":"Confirm the valve is set to SEALING. Pressure Cook (Manual) on High for 3 minutes, natural release 10 minutes, then quick release. Fluff and leave the lid off to cool.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"8C341A29-7BAD-548F-8701-DA689E0C07FF","order":13,"step":"Microwave the frozen broccoli in its bags per the package. It is already blanched, so it needs steaming rather than cooking. Tip it into a colander and shake off the water.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"947B64C4-DCE2-5041-B080-156F563C4434","order":14,"step":"Cool the chicken, rice, and broccoli 10–15 minutes before portioning. Sealing hot food causes condensation and sogginess.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"18089296-4EF6-585B-9DD8-3720C6B89DFC","order":15,"step":"Assembly line across 10 open containers: 200 g sauced chicken, then 148 g rice, then 127 g broccoli. That 200 g is 148 g of meat plus 52 g of sauce — both measured, not inferred. No separate sauce container; the cornstarch is what makes that safe.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"6DC640C8-0351-5C4E-94EF-176839EA45B1","order":16,"step":"Label with a dry-erase marker. 6 containers to the fridge, 4 to the freezer. Move freezer containers to the fridge the night before eating.","isSection":false}],"notes":"TESTING — v6, last cooked 2026-11-01\n\nPER CONTAINER (10 total)\n  chicken: 200 g\n  rice: 148 g\n  broccoli: 127 g\n  total: 475 g\n\nSTORAGE: 6 fridge, 4 freezer\nFreezer containers move to the fridge the night before eating.\n\nFRESH TOPPINGS — NEVER PACKED: sliced scallions, toasted sesame seeds, sriracha\nAdded at eating time only.\n\nCooked 2026-11-01, the first time this recipe has existed outside arithmetic.\nChicken, rice and sauce are now observed:\n\n  chicken    6.06 lb package -> 1481 g shredded, unsauced   (0.539)\n  sauce      measured at 520 g, not estimated\n  rice       3 ½ cups dry    -> 1613 g                      (461 g per dry cup)\n\n`testing` rather than `dialed-in`, because that cook deviated from this recipe\nin two ways. It used fresh broccoli instead of the frozen specified here, and\n3 ½ cups of rice instead of 3 ¼. The protein and the sauce are settled; the\nvegetable as written still has not been through a cook in this recipe. One run\nwith the frozen bags promotes it.\n\n**The bottled sauce is gone, and with it the reason this recipe never got\ncooked.** Two bottles put it at ~1400 mg of sodium and 12 g of added sugar —\nnearly double the ceiling and the worst numbers in the rotation. The scratch\nsauce is the same soy-and-aromatics build that `ground-beef-broccoli` has\nalready proven at ~750 mg, with chicken broth in place of beef and a third\ntablespoon of brown sugar, because teriyaki wants more sweetness than beef and\nbroccoli does. Projects at ~720 mg and 4 g of added sugar.\n\nThat projection carries the same warning the beef recipe carries:\n**low-sodium soy is load-bearing.** ⅔ cup of regular soy would land this near\n975 mg, over the ceiling. If Aldi only has regular, use ½ cup and add ¼ cup\nmore broth to hold the sauce volume. Do not swap one for one.\n\n**Frozen broccoli replaces 5 ½ lbs of fresh florets**, which is where most of\nthe old 40-minute active time went: washing, cutting, oiling, two 12–15 minute\nroasting batches, and a sheet pan to scrub. Buffalo measured frozen florets at\n0.931 against 0.377–0.481 for fresh crowns, so it is both far less work and\nfar more predictable. 4 bags rather than buffalo's 3, because this recipe has\nalways run a heavier vegetable portion.\n\nThe 2026-11-01 cook tested fresh crowns in the new air fryer and the numbers\nback the frozen spec: 7.49 lbs of crowns yielded 0.399, giving 1356 g where\n4 frozen bags give ~1266 g — a marginal gain for roughly 15 minutes of extra\nknife work, a higher price, and three times the raw weight to carry home.\nTaste is the only argument fresh has left, and that is not recorded yet.\n\n**The sauce is no longer packed separately.** v4 used ten small side\ncontainers because bottled teriyaki is thin and would soak the rice. A\ncornstarch-thickened sauce clings to shredded chicken instead of running off\nit — the same reason the beef recipe packs no sauce either. That removes ten\ncontainers to fill, label and wash, and it retires the open question in v4\nabout whether 42 g or 50 g per bowl was right. The question no longer exists.\n\nTogether those three changes take active time from 40 minutes to about 20,\nwhich would make this the second low-effort recipe in the rotation. Buffalo is\ncurrently the only one, and the next easiest cookable recipe is 45 minutes.\n\n`per_container.chicken` is **sauced** weight, following buffalo, and here both\nhalves are measured rather than inferred: 148 g of meat plus 52 g of sauce.\nBuffalo had to estimate its own sauce and never confirmed it, so 520 g is the\nfirst real figure this repo has for a scratch sauce of this volume — 16%\nheavier than the ~450 g projected.\n\nThe buy quantity rose from 5 ¾ lbs to 6 because the crockpot yield keeps\nfalling. The three sessions read 0.637, 0.572 and now 0.539 in date order,\nwhich is a trend rather than scatter, and standing parameters now plans at\n0.54. The overshoot on this cook hid the drop entirely — 6.06 lbs at 0.539\nreturned 1481 g, where 5 ¾ lbs at the old 0.57 predicted 1487 g. Buying 5%\nlong absorbed a yield 5% short.\n\nNote that `ground-beef-broccoli` counts its protein *unsauced* — 185 g of beef\nwith the sauce uncounted on top. The two recipes genuinely disagree about what\n`per_container` means. Buffalo's convention is the one to keep, because it is\nthe number you can actually put on a scale, and the beef recipe should be\nreconciled to it on its next cook rather than guessed at now.\n\nAt ~533 kcal this is the lightest bowl in the rotation, a consequence of lean\nshredded breast and a sauce that is mostly soy and broth rather than oil. The\n2026-11-01 cook ran heavier at ~554 kcal because it used 3 ½ cups of rice and\n136 g of broccoli; both are fine, and 3 ¼ cups is the spec only because it\nmatches buffalo and keeps the carbs down. Either is defensible — pick one and\nrecord which.\n\nReheats in a microwave, which is the only thing available at work. Rice and\nchicken freeze well; the broccoli softens slightly but holds.","neutritionalInfo":"Serving Size: 1 container,\nCalories: 533 kcal,\nProtein: 53 g,\nCarbohydrates: 56 g,\nFat: 7 g,\nFiber: 4 g,\nSodium: 720 mg,\nSugar: 4 g","sourceName":"ten-containers","webLink":"https://zjbecker32.github.io/ten-containers/recipes/teriyaki-chicken/","tags":[],"images":[]}